) is the number of observations in each group.\n\n#### Design Layout\nThe SNK test is typically applied after a one-way ANOVA, where the experimental design involves multiple groups (treatments) being compared. The groups should be independent, and the data should be normally distributed with homogeneity of variances.\n\n#### Assumptions\nFor the SNK test to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The samples must be independent.\n2. The data within each group should be normally distributed.\n3. The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n#### Applicability and Selection Criteria\nThe SNK test is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher has conducted an ANOVA and found significant differences among group means.\n- The number of groups is moderate (typically less than 10), as the test becomes less reliable with a larger number of groups due to increased Type I error rates.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure involves the following steps:\n1. Conduct a one-way ANOVA to test for overall differences among group means.\n2. If the ANOVA is significant, apply the SNK test to perform pairwise comparisons of group means.\n3. Calculate the critical difference and compare the means of each pair of groups to determine which means are significantly different.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- The SNK test is relatively simple to compute and interpret.\n- It controls the Type I error rate effectively when the number of comparisons is not excessively large.\n- It provides a clear method for identifying specific group differences.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The SNK test can be overly liberal (leading to increased Type I error rates) when the number of groups is large.\n- It assumes equal sample sizes, which can be a limitation in practical applications where sample sizes may vary.\n- The test is sensitive to violations of the normality and homogeneity of variance assumptions.\n\n#### Related Designs and Alternatives